+# Trusted HA bridge candidate — NOT DEPLOYED
+
+Version: 0.1.70-haipc1. Base: upstream v0.1.70, commit 06ee7b0b77455119a0ffcbc79217e6ccda8a7128.
+
+Reuse the existing private /data/verification.sock and active task capability. The helper never reads HA credentials or uses HTTP. The trusted parent performs only five fixed operations and eleven codex_cli services. Browser identity stays read-only. No new MCP broker or transport server is introduced. Supervisor credentials remain in the parent environment. The existing subprocess environment no longer injects optional HA_TOKEN/HA_URL.
+
+Request bound: 16384 bytes. Reply bound: 2 MiB. API timeout: 15 seconds; helper timeout: 40 seconds. No redirects, proxies, arbitrary URLs, shell execution or retries. Mutation IDs are serialized and deduplicated in a bounded process-local ledger. An unknown outcome must be read back manually before any fresh mutation. IDs do not survive a worker restart; the helper does not retry across restarts. Core restart is explicitly disabled by parent policy; the restart-core command returns PERMISSION_DENIED without any Supervisor call. Audit emits START/RESULT summaries to existing container stderr, not a new unbounded file.
+
+## Core restart disabled by user decision
+Supervisor 2026.09.3 supervisor/api/middleware/security.py allows default role only paths ending /info; POST /core/restart is denied. homeassistant role allows /core/* but exceeds the requested default role. The candidate keeps default and does NOT broaden it. The explicit restart command is retained for a clear denial, but the backend performs no restart HTTP request. This is a deliberate policy, not a deployment blocker. No role upgrade is requested. Source: https://github.com/home-assistant/supervisor/blob/2026.09.3/supervisor/api/middleware/security.py
+
+## External build and validation
+No builder or supported add-on deployment/restart API is available in the current execution context. No image, digest, deployment or worker restart has been produced.
+
+1. On an external isolated runner, run: HA_BRIDGE_REAL_IPC_TEST=1 python -B -m unittest discover -s bridge-tests. Run worker server regression tests and remaining upstream suite with its dependencies.
+2. Resolve an appropriate amd64 Home Assistant Alpine base image to its sha256 digest. Build from codex-cli-worker with BUILD_FROM pinned to that digest, BUILD_VERSION=0.1.70-haipc1, BUILD_ARCH=amd64 and CODEX_VERSION=0.160.0. Record resulting image digest. Do not claim a reproducible digest before a successful build. Example command, substitute actual pinned base reference:
+   docker build --build-arg BUILD_FROM=<base-image@sha256:digest> --build-arg BUILD_VERSION=0.1.70-haipc1 --build-arg BUILD_ARCH=amd64 --build-arg CODEX_VERSION=0.160.0 -t <private-image>:0.1.70-haipc1 codex-cli-worker
+3. Exercise image startup, Python imports, CLI startup, real IPC, authentication denial, task cancellation, read verification, config_check and dashboard pipeline in isolation. Never inject a real Supervisor token into test child processes. Confirm effective add-on permissions through supported Supervisor metadata.
+4. Before installation, checkpoint the actual installed worker image/digest, add-on config, options, data/pairing, and recovery path using supported HA backup mechanisms. They are not accessible here; the upstream declaration is not a backup of the installed add-on.
+5. Install only through a supported custom add-on/image workflow preserving pairing/data. Restart ONLY the worker as authorized. Do not install or edit codex_cli backend and do not restart Core. Read-only smoke tests: ha-codex-ha ping, config, services. Verify child credentials absent. Missing eleven registry services is recorded, not repaired by an automatic Core restart.
+6. Rollback: restore the original 0.1.70 worker image, config/options and pairing checkpoint through the same supported workflow; restart only worker. The local helper backup is in this task turn backups/bin/ha-codex-ha. Never edit Supervisor or HA .storage by hand.
+
+No service writes or Core restart are part of these smoke tests. Core restart remains disabled. Any future enablement requires a new explicit decision and security review.
